// app:display — the display server.
//
// "Server" is Godot parlance. This is not Godot's server architecture; it
// approximates the *surface* of Godot's DisplayServer (see
// Godot's own, held only as reference material) over vendor:sokol.
//
// This file is the whole public interface — the window/mouse/clipboard/timing
// surface and the run entry. The private sokol machinery (the sokol callbacks
// and the clear colour) lives in `src/` and is not imported from anywhere but
// here; see `src/backend.dado`.
//
// Two deliberate differences from Godot's DisplayServer, both forced by the
// substrate:
//   * It is single-window. sokol_app is a single-window library.
//   * It also owns the frame loop entry, because sokol_app fuses window
//     creation with the loop — sapp_run is the only way to open the window.
//
// **`Config`, `Hooks`, `run`, `begin_pass` and `end_pass` are the seam
// `app:graphical` drives, and a program should not call them.** Write a program
// against `app:app` + `app:graphical`: `graphical.new(w, h, title)` installs a
// real `app.Backend` on the singleton and `app.run()` opens the window. The
// seam stays *here* rather than moving into `graphical` for one reason: sokol's
// window handle, its `sapp_desc` and its `^const sapp_event` are the substrate
// this package exists to be the only importer of, and exporting the loop entry
// would export them with it. Everything below `run` is ordinary
// public surface a program may call from `ready`/`process`.
package display

import "vendor:sokol/sokol_app"
import "core:strings"
import "core:mem/libc"
import backend "./src"

// ── Godot-approximating enums ──────────────────────────────────────────────

// Window presentation mode. sokol_app exposes only windowed vs fullscreen;
// Godot's MINIMIZED / MAXIMIZED / EXCLUSIVE_FULLSCREEN have no sokol surface.
enum WindowMode: (Windowed, Fullscreen)

// Pointer mode, after Godot's MouseMode. sokol supports these three; Godot's
// CONFINED / CONFINED_HIDDEN have no sokol surface.
enum MouseMode: (Visible, Hidden, Captured)

// Cursor shape — the subset of Godot's CursorShape that sokol_app provides. The
// backing values are sokol's own sapp_mouse_cursor numbers, so the sokol call
// needs no lookup table: `cursor_set_shape` converts the number straight across
// rather than mapping member to member.
enum u32 CursorShape:
    Arrow = 1,
    Ibeam = 2,
    Crosshair = 3,
    PointingHand = 4,
    ResizeEW = 5,
    ResizeNS = 6,
    ResizeNWSE = 7,
    ResizeNESW = 8,
    ResizeAll = 9,
    NotAllowed = 10,

// ── Opening the display ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// How to open the window.
//
//   width, height           the window's size, in logical pixels
//   title                   the window title
//   max_dropped_files       how many file paths one drop may land. **`0` turns
//                           drag-and-drop off**, which is what
//                           `sapp_desc.enable_dragndrop` becomes.
//   max_dropped_path_bytes  the longest path sokol will accept, terminator
//                           included. Read only when `max_dropped_files` is
//                           above 0.
//
// **Drops are off by default and the default is the safe one.** At `0` the
// window sets no `XdndAware` property at all, so no drag source ever offers it
// a drop, and a program that has written no drop handling *declines* the
// gesture rather than swallowing it. Measured under `Xvfb`: with the flag
// unset `XGetWindowProperty(XdndAware)` answers `nitems=0`; with it set the
// same window answers `version=5` and completes a real
// `XdndEnter`/`XdndPosition`/`XdndDrop` handshake. A window that advertises
// drops and then drops them on the floor is the worse of the two, because the
// file vanishes either way and only that one looked like it would work.
//
// **A `Config` field and not a `@const` build knob** — the shape
// `CLIPBOARD_BYTES` takes. A buffer size is a build's business; this is a
// *behaviour* a program either implements or does not, and two programs in one
// build differ on it. `graphical.set_file_drop` is what a program calls; this
// is the seat it reaches, and it costs one tuple literal because `Config` is
// constructed in exactly one place in the tree (`run_loop`, `app:graphical`).
type Config: (i32 width, i32 height, string8 title,
              i32 max_dropped_files, i32 max_dropped_path_bytes)

// Lifecycle callbacks `run` invokes. Any may be nil.
//
// **Four, and none of them carries an allocator.** The five-hook shape this
// replaces had a `draw` phase of its own and passed a per-frame `Allocator`
// that `display` owned and reset — a second runtime beside `app:app`'s, with a
// second scratch arena and an incompatible hook shape. `app:app`'s three hooks
// and its ambient `app.scratch()` won, because that is what every other `app:`
// package and every example is written against; these four are the *seam*
// underneath them, not a rival to them.
//
//   ready  — once, after graphics are up
//   frame  — once per frame; dt is seconds since the last frame. What a frame
//            *is* — the pass bracket, the barrier, the scratch reset — is the
//            caller's, not this package's.
//   event  — once per input event; the payload is the raw `sapp_event` address
//   finish — once, on shutdown, before sg_shutdown
type Hooks: (
    void() ready,
    void(f32) frame,
    void(rawptr) event,
    void() finish,
)

// Open the window described by `cfg`, install `hooks`, and run the frame loop
// until the window closes. Blocks until then.
void run(Config cfg, Hooks hooks):
    backend.run(cfg.width, cfg.height, cfg.title, CLIPBOARD_BYTES,
                cfg.max_dropped_files, cfg.max_dropped_path_bytes,
                hooks.ready, hooks.frame, hooks.event, hooks.finish)

// ── The render pass ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two calls rather than a `draw` hook, so the caller chooses what sits between
// them. `app:graphical` puts them in `app.Backend.begin_frame`/`end_frame`,
// which is the bracket `app:app` already wraps around `process`.

// Begin the default swapchain pass, clearing to the background colour.
void begin_pass():
    backend.begin_pass()

// End the pass and present the frame.
void end_pass():
    backend.end_pass()

// ── Surface: background ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The colour the frame is cleared to each frame (0..1 per channel).
void set_background(f32 r, g, b, a):
    backend.set_background(r, g, b, a)

// ── Surface: window ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

i32 window_get_width():
    return sokol_app.sapp_width()

i32 window_get_height():
    return sokol_app.sapp_height()

(i32, i32) window_get_size():
    return (sokol_app.sapp_width(), sokol_app.sapp_height())

f32 window_get_dpi_scale():
    return sokol_app.sapp_dpi_scale()

bool window_is_high_dpi():
    return sokol_app.sapp_high_dpi()

void window_set_title(string8 title):
    ref []char8 tc = strings.clone_to_cstring(title, libc.allocator())
    defer #delete(tc)
    sokol_app.sapp_set_window_title(cstring(tc))

WindowMode window_get_mode():
    if sokol_app.sapp_is_fullscreen():
        return WindowMode.Fullscreen
    return WindowMode.Windowed

void window_set_mode(WindowMode mode):
    bool want_fs = mode == WindowMode.Fullscreen
    if sokol_app.sapp_is_fullscreen() != want_fs:
        sokol_app.sapp_toggle_fullscreen()

// ── Surface: mouse & cursor ────────────────────────────────────────────────

void mouse_set_mode(MouseMode mode):
    switch mode:
        case MouseMode.Visible:
            sokol_app.sapp_show_mouse(true)
            sokol_app.sapp_lock_mouse(false)
        case MouseMode.Hidden:
            sokol_app.sapp_show_mouse(false)
            sokol_app.sapp_lock_mouse(false)
        case MouseMode.Captured:
            sokol_app.sapp_show_mouse(false)
            sokol_app.sapp_lock_mouse(true)
        else:
            return

MouseMode mouse_get_mode():
    if sokol_app.sapp_mouse_locked():
        return MouseMode.Captured
    if sokol_app.sapp_mouse_shown():
        return MouseMode.Visible
    return MouseMode.Hidden

void cursor_set_shape(CursorShape shape):
    sokol_app.sapp_set_mouse_cursor(sokol_app.sapp_mouse_cursor(u32(shape)))

// The cursors a UI toolkit asks for by name, as plain `i32`s so a widget's
// hook can answer one without importing this package's enum: the system's
// default pointer, the text I-beam, the pointing hand over something that
// acts when clicked, and the two splitter arrows. `app:ui` re-exports them.
const i32 CURSOR_DEFAULT   = 0
const i32 CURSOR_IBEAM     = 1
const i32 CURSOR_POINTER   = 2
const i32 CURSOR_RESIZE_EW = 3
const i32 CURSOR_RESIZE_NS = 4

// The `sapp_mouse_cursor` number a `CURSOR_*` names. `CURSOR_DEFAULT`, and
// any number that is none of the five, is `SAPP_MOUSECURSOR_DEFAULT` — the
// platform's own arrow, which `CursorShape` has no member for (its `Arrow` is
// sokol's explicit arrow, not "whatever the system shows"). Pure, so the
// mapping is checkable with no window.
u32 cursor_sapp_value(i32 cursor):
    if cursor == CURSOR_IBEAM:
        return u32(CursorShape.Ibeam)
    if cursor == CURSOR_POINTER:
        return u32(CursorShape.PointingHand)
    if cursor == CURSOR_RESIZE_EW:
        return u32(CursorShape.ResizeEW)
    if cursor == CURSOR_RESIZE_NS:
        return u32(CursorShape.ResizeNS)
    return 0

// Show `cursor` (a `CURSOR_*`) over the window. **A no-op with no window
// open** — headless, in a test, before `run` or after it returns — rather
// than sokol's assertion on uninitialised state. sokol itself skips a call
// that names the cursor already shown, so a caller that repeats one pays a
// comparison; `app:ui` calls this only when the effective cursor changes.
void set_cursor(i32 cursor):
    if !sokol_app.sapp_isvalid():
        return
    sokol_app.sapp_set_mouse_cursor(sokol_app.sapp_mouse_cursor(cursor_sapp_value(cursor)))

// ── Surface: clipboard ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **The clipboard is a fixed buffer, and it used to truncate silently (E6).**
// sokol allocates `sapp_desc.clipboard_size` bytes once, inside `sapp_run`, and
// `sapp_set_clipboard_string` copies into it with a bounded `strcpy`: a longer
// string was cut to fit, the tail was lost, and the only report was a
// `CLIPBOARD_STRING_TOO_BIG` line on **sokol's** logger, which nothing in Dado
// reads. Measured by `example/edit`: select-all on a 19 551-byte file, copy,
// paste — **8 191 bytes arrived**, with `clipboard_set` having answered
// nothing at all. The client's only recourse was to set, read back, and
// compare lengths.
//
// Two changes: `clipboard_set` answers `bool`, and it refuses rather than
// truncates — a clipboard holding the first 40% of a selection is worse than a
// clipboard that still holds what it had, because the user pastes it. And
// `clipboard_capacity()` says how much fits, so a program can decide in advance
// instead of discovering it afterwards.

// How many bytes `clipboard_set` will accept, not counting the terminator.
//
// A build constant, not a runtime one: sokol allocates the buffer inside
// `sapp_run` from `sapp_desc.clipboard_size` and never resizes it, so any
// runtime setter would be a lie everywhere except before the window opens.
i32 clipboard_capacity():
    return CLIPBOARD_BYTES - 1

// The clipboard buffer sokol is asked for, in bytes.
//
// **8 192 was sokol's default and it is far too small**: it is 8 KB of text —
// an editor exceeds it on a nine-kilobyte file, which is a small source file.
// The buffer is one `malloc` at window creation that lives for the process, so
// the whole cost of being generous is a megabyte of resident memory in a
// program that has already advertised `Clipboard` and is holding a GPU context;
// `app:draw`'s vertex buffer alone is the same order. A megabyte holds roughly
// 25 000 lines of source, which is past where "select all and copy" is a thing
// a person does to a text file.
//
// **A `@const` and not a parameter**, for two reasons. It cannot be changed
// after the window opens, so the only honest runtime home for it would be an
// argument to `graphical.new(w, h, title)` — the one line every graphical
// program is required to write — which would tax every program in the tree for
// a knob almost none of them turn. And `@const` is already this collection's
// established build knob: `-D display.CLIPBOARD_BYTES=65536` on an embedded
// target is the same shape as `-D sokol_gfx.BACKEND=dummy`, and costs no
// public surface at all.
@const i32 CLIPBOARD_BYTES = 1048576

// Whether `clipboard_set` would take `text` whole, the window aside: it is
// no longer than `clipboard_capacity()`, and it holds no NUL byte. **A NUL is
// refused for the same reason an oversize string is:** the text reaches sokol
// as a C string, which ends at the first NUL, so everything after it would be
// dropped while the call answered that it landed — and an editor cutting a
// selection trusts that answer before it removes the text. Decided from the
// string alone, so a program can ask before it tries, and so the rule is
// checkable with no window.
bool clipboard_accepts(string8 text):
    i64 n = #len(text)
    if n > i64(clipboard_capacity()):
        return false
    for i in 0..<n:
        if u8(text[i]) == 0:
            return false
    return true

// Put `text` on the system clipboard. **Answers whether it landed.**
//
// `false` means nothing was written and the clipboard still holds whatever it
// held, for one of three reasons: the string is longer than
// `clipboard_capacity()`, it holds a NUL byte (see `clipboard_accepts`), or
// the display is not open yet (sokol's clipboard calls are silent no-ops
// before `sapp_run` and after it returns).
bool clipboard_set(string8 text):
    if !sokol_app.sapp_isvalid():
        return false
    if !clipboard_accepts(text):
        return false
    ref []char8 tc = strings.clone_to_cstring(text, libc.allocator())
    defer #delete(tc)
    sokol_app.sapp_set_clipboard_string(cstring(tc))
    return true

// Returns a `cstring`, not a `string`, matching core:os.env: a C string cannot
// become a Dado string without walking and owning it. Measure or print it
// (strings.measure, println), or `strings.clone_from_cstring` for an owned copy.
//
// Bounded by the same buffer: a selection put on the clipboard by another
// program that is longer than `clipboard_capacity()` arrives truncated, and
// sokol gives no way to tell that from a selection that was exactly that long.
// Raising `CLIPBOARD_BYTES` is the only lever there is.
cstring clipboard_get():
    return sokol_app.sapp_get_clipboard_string()

// ── Surface: lifecycle & timing ────────────────────────────────────────────

// Ask the window to close; the loop finishes the frame, runs cleanup, `run`
// returns.
void request_close():
    sokol_app.sapp_request_quit()

// Cancel a close that a quit-requested event began.
void cancel_close():
    sokol_app.sapp_cancel_quit()

// Close now, without the quit-requested handshake.
void quit():
    sokol_app.sapp_quit()

// Whether the display is initialised and the graphics context is valid.
bool valid():
    return sokol_app.sapp_isvalid()

// True only inside the render pass (between `begin_pass` and `end_pass`) — so,
// under `app:graphical`, true for the whole of `app:app`'s `process` hook and
// false everywhere else. The app:draw primitives check this and panic if called
// outside the frame.
bool drawing():
    return backend.drawing()

u64 frame_count():
    return sokol_app.sapp_frame_count()

// Seconds the last frame took (smoothed).
f64 frame_duration():
    return sokol_app.sapp_frame_duration()
